  <section id="average" aria-labelledby="average-h">
    <h2 id="average-h">Average Thigh Size for Men and Women</h2>
    <p>Thigh size depends heavily on <em>where</em> you put the tape. At the <strong>upper thigh</strong>, just below the buttock, the average is about <strong>24.3&nbsp;inches (61.6&nbsp;cm) for women</strong> and <strong>24.6&nbsp;inches (62.5&nbsp;cm) for men</strong> (U.S. Army survey, ANSUR&nbsp;II). At <strong>mid-thigh</strong>, halfway to the knee, U.S. adults average <strong>20.8&nbsp;inches for women</strong> and <strong>21.2&nbsp;inches for men</strong> (NHANES).</p>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Upper-thigh percentiles (ANSUR II)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">5th</th><th scope="col">25th</th><th scope="col">50th</th><th scope="col">75th</th><th scope="col">95th</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Women</th><td>20.6 in (52.4)</td><td>22.8 in (57.8)</td><td>24.3 in (61.6)</td><td>25.7 in (65.4)</td><td>27.9 in (70.8)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Men</th><td>20.9 in (53.0)</td><td>23.1 in (58.6)</td><td>24.6 in (62.5)</td><td>26.1 in (66.4)</td><td>28.4 in (72.0)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>Unlike most body measurements, thigh size is almost the same for men and women. Men have more muscle in the thigh, women more fat, and the two balance out on the tape measure.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="women" aria-labelledby="women-h">
    <h2 id="women-h">Thigh Size Chart for Women</h2>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/thigh-size-chart-women-3d.webp" width="1600" height="980" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Thigh size chart for women: 3D figures with 20 to 28 inch upper thighs and their percentiles"><figcaption>A 5&nbsp;ft&nbsp;5&nbsp;in woman with upper-thigh sizes from 20 to 28 inches.</figcaption></figure>
    <p>For women, thigh size tracks hip size very closely; in the Army data, hip circumference explains almost 90% of the variation in thigh size. If your thighs are large compared with your bust and waist, you may have a <a href="/pear-body-shape/">pear body shape</a>, the most common shape in women.</p>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Women: upper thigh by height (ANSUR II)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Height</th><th scope="col">Average</th><th scope="col">Middle 90%</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Under 5 ft 2 in</th><td>23.3 in (59.3 cm)</td><td>20.1&ndash;26.9 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 2&ndash;4 in</th><td>24.1 in (61.1 cm)</td><td>20.7&ndash;27.5 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 5&ndash;7 in</th><td>24.8 in (63.0 cm)</td><td>21.4&ndash;28.3 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 8 in and over</th><td>25.8 in (65.4 cm)</td><td>22.5&ndash;28.8 in</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>

  <section id="men" aria-labelledby="men-h">
    <h2 id="men-h">Thigh Size Chart for Men</h2>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/thigh-size-chart-men-3d.webp" width="1600" height="980" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Thigh size chart for men: 3D figures with 20 to 28 inch upper thighs and their percentiles"><figcaption>A 5&nbsp;ft&nbsp;10&nbsp;in man with upper-thigh sizes from 20 to 28 inches.</figcaption></figure>
    <p>For men, bigger thighs usually mean more muscle, especially in the quadriceps. Recreational lifters often measure 24&ndash;26&nbsp;inches at the upper thigh, while competitive powerlifters and bodybuilders can exceed 28&nbsp;inches.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ranks" aria-labelledby="ranks-h">
    <h2 id="ranks-h">Is Your Thigh Size Big? Common Sizes Explained</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Where upper-thigh sizes rank (percentile, ANSUR II)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Upper thigh</th><th scope="col">Women</th><th scope="col">Men</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">20 in (51 cm)</th><td>3rd</td><td>2nd</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">21 in (53 cm)</th><td>7th</td><td>6th</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">22 in (56 cm)</th><td>15th</td><td>13th</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">23 in (58 cm)</th><td>29th</td><td>24th</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">24 in (61 cm)</th><td>45th</td><td>40th</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">25 in (64 cm)</th><td>63rd</td><td>57th</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">26 in (66 cm)</th><td>79th</td><td>73rd</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">27 in (69 cm)</th><td>89th</td><td>85th</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">28 in (71 cm)</th><td>96th</td><td>93rd</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">30 in (76 cm)</th><td>99th</td><td>99th</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>These ranks are for the upper thigh. The same reading taken at mid-thigh ranks much higher, because mid-thigh is several inches smaller on the same leg. Always check which site a chart uses.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="by-age" aria-labelledby="by-age-h">
    <h2 id="by-age-h">Average Thigh Size by Age</h2>
    <p>Thigh size stays fairly steady through the 20s to 40s, then shrinks as people lose muscle in later life. This mid-thigh data comes from the NHANES 2003&ndash;2006 survey, the most recent national survey to measure the thigh.</p>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Average mid-thigh circumference by age (NHANES 2003&ndash;2006)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Age</th><th scope="col">Men</th><th scope="col">Women</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">20&ndash;29</th><td>21.7 in (55.1 cm)</td><td>20.8 in (52.9 cm)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">30&ndash;39</th><td>21.7 in (55.1 cm)</td><td>21.3 in (54.2 cm)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">40&ndash;49</th><td>21.7 in (55.0 cm)</td><td>21.4 in (54.4 cm)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">50&ndash;59</th><td>20.9 in (53.2 cm)</td><td>20.9 in (53.2 cm)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">60&ndash;69</th><td>20.4 in (51.9 cm)</td><td>20.6 in (52.2 cm)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">70&ndash;79</th><td>19.7 in (50.0 cm)</td><td>19.5 in (49.6 cm)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">80+</th><td>18.3 in (46.6 cm)</td><td>18.7 in (47.5 cm)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>The drop after 60, about 3 to 4&nbsp;inches by the 80s, mostly reflects muscle loss (sarcopenia). Leg strength training is one of the most effective ways to slow it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-measure" aria-labelledby="how-to-measure-h">
    <h2 id="how-to-measure-h">How to Measure Your Thigh</h2>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/how-to-measure-thigh-circumference-3d.webp" width="1400" height="860" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Where to measure thigh circumference: upper thigh, mid-thigh and above the knee on a 3D man and woman"><figcaption>Upper thigh, mid-thigh and above-knee tape positions.</figcaption></figure>
    <ol class="tp-steps"><li><strong>Stand relaxed.</strong> Stand with feet about hip-width apart and your weight spread evenly on both legs. Keep the leg muscles relaxed.</li><li><strong>Find the spot.</strong> For the upper thigh, find the widest point just below the buttock crease. For mid-thigh, mark halfway between the groin crease and the top of the kneecap.</li><li><strong>Wrap the tape.</strong> Wrap a soft tape around the thigh at that point, keeping it level all the way around. It should sit flat on the skin without pressing in.</li><li><strong>Read and repeat.</strong> Read the tape, take a second reading and use the average. Note which leg you measured so you can compare like with like later.</li></ol>
    <p>See all the other tape positions in our <a href="/body-measurements/">body measurements guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="health" aria-labelledby="health-h">
    <h2 id="health-h">Thigh Size and Health</h2>
    <p>Thigh size says something about muscle. A Danish study of about 2,800 adults followed for over 12 years found that people with a thigh circumference below about 60&nbsp;cm (23.6&nbsp;in, measured just below the buttock) had a higher risk of heart disease and early death, even after accounting for body fat. Small thighs often signal low muscle mass.</p>
    <p>Large thighs are not a problem in themselves. Fat stored on the hips and thighs is less harmful than fat around the waist, which is why <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist-to-hip ratio</a> gives a better picture of risk than thigh size alone. For overall fatness, check the <a href="/body-fat-percentage-chart/">body fat percentage chart</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="change" aria-labelledby="change-h">
    <h2 id="change-h">Can You Change Your Thigh Size?</h2>
    <p><strong>Smaller thighs:</strong> losing body fat overall is the only way to slim the thighs; exercises that target the inner or outer thigh build muscle but do not burn the fat over it. Many women lose thigh fat later than belly fat because hormones favor fat storage there.</p>
    <p><strong>Bigger thighs:</strong> squats, lunges, Romanian deadlifts, leg presses and hip thrusts build the quads, hamstrings and glutes. Train legs at least twice a week and add weight over time. Want to see the effect first? Change the thigh measurement in the <a href="/body-visualizer/">3D body visualizer</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Thigh Size FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the average thigh size for a woman?</h3></summary><p>Measured at mid-thigh, the average U.S. woman’s thigh is about 20.8 inches (52.9 cm), from NHANES data for women aged 20 and over. Measured at the upper thigh just below the buttock, U.S. Army women average 24.3 inches (61.6 cm).</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the average thigh size for a man?</h3></summary><p>At mid-thigh, the average U.S. man’s thigh is about 21.2 inches (53.8 cm) according to NHANES. At the upper thigh, U.S. Army men average 24.6 inches (62.5 cm).</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is a 22 inch thigh big?</h3></summary><p>At the upper thigh, 22 inches is below average for both sexes (about the 15th percentile for women and the 13th for men). At mid-thigh, 22 inches is above average, larger than about 69% of U.S. women.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is a 24 inch thigh big?</h3></summary><p>At the upper thigh, 24 inches is close to average: about the 45th percentile for women and the 40th for men. At mid-thigh it is large, bigger than about 86% of U.S. women.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Why are women’s thighs similar in size to men’s?</h3></summary><p>Women carry more fat on the hips and thighs, while men carry more muscle there. The two roughly cancel out, so average thigh circumference is nearly the same for both sexes even though men are taller and heavier.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Where should I measure my thigh?</h3></summary><p>For clothing and most size charts, measure the upper thigh at its widest point, just below the buttock crease. For comparing with health surveys, measure at mid-thigh, halfway between the groin crease and the top of the kneecap. Always measure the same leg while standing relaxed.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Are bigger thighs healthier?</h3></summary><p>Thigh size from muscle is linked with better health. Several studies have found that people with very small thighs have a higher risk of heart disease and diabetes, likely because small thighs reflect low muscle mass. Large thighs from fat do not carry the same benefit, so waist size and body fat still matter.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How can I make my thighs smaller or bigger?</h3></summary><p>You cannot spot-reduce fat from the thighs; losing overall body fat is what shrinks them. To build bigger thighs, train the quads, hamstrings and glutes with squats, lunges, leg presses and deadlifts, progressing the load over time and eating enough protein.</p></details>
  </section>

